i'm joined today by gretchen baki who is  author of the grid the fraying wires  between Americans and our energy future  Gretchen it's so great to talk to you  know we talk so much about the move  towards alternative and renewable  sources of energy we talk about the  political implications and and the sort  of economic implications but one thing  that's often missing from that  conversation is sort of the reality of  the electrical grid that were working  with and some of the limitations that  exist and we'll sort of have to be dealt  with if we really want to move away from  fossil fuels which I believe that we  should be trying to do let's start as  broadly as you want to I mean what are  some of the limitations that need to be  considered I think the main thing that  people don't quite grasp when there's  when when when thinking about the energy  transition is that electricity can't  really be stored right now and what that  means is that as it's made its used it's  this very instantaneous product so as  it's made its used and that means that  if we make it with something like coal  it's a very steady production and if we  make it with something like wind it's a  very unsteady production because the  amount of electricity changes as the as  the rate of the wind blowing changes and  so that's called variable generation and  it's the more of it that we put into our  grid we tend to think of it as  renewables but not all renewables are  variable and the more we put of it we  put into the grid the the harder it is  to manage this sort of perfect balancing  of how much electricity we use in any  instant and how much electricity we  produce in that same instant because  electricity is essentially instantaneous  so I'm almost never more than a minute  between when it's made and when it's  used and this is particularly relevant  for people who already have or are  thinking of moving to solar power in  their home unless you actually have a  means of storing electricity right  unless you have an actual battery set up  any excess electricity that is harvested  by your home solar system is not stored  for your future use but rather goes back  into the grid and as you  is almost instant state instantaneously  used by someone else I mean is that  generally the way that it works yeah and  I think that's one of the things that I  was really confused about for a long  time was why people could have diesel  generators and seemed to be like when  there was a blackout they would be able  to power things in their house but if  you have solar panels you can't and like  many people I thought that was something  that the utilities work on somehow  scheming to make it that you so you  couldn't go off the grid completely but  actually diesel generators have all  these very heavy pieces inside of them  and so when you turn on a light switch  there's a kind of inertial force inside  the generator that allows it to balance  your house so the the power you have on  or turning on or turning off in any  moment it has to be balanced by the  amount of electricity that generator is  producing so again the fossil fuel and  then this heavy piece of equipment  enable you to run your house even  without storage where solar panels  obviously you're not going to have any  any power at night but even so you turn  on your light there's no way for our  source for a solar panel to adjust to  that without some kind of storage so you  turn on a light you're asking for more  electricity from that system but that  system is just producing electricity  based on how much Sun is hitting it at  that moment and you know we don't use we  don't even think in terms of like okay  it's midday and it's really sunny so I'm  going to run everything right now right  our fridge turns on or freezer turns on  it goes off we turn on the lights we  turn them off and all of that has to be  balanced in some way by something and  right now that something is the grid and  people like the grid it's not there's  not a there's not really a strong  movement I think against the grid but  there is this sense of like why can't I  be energy independent and the reason for  that is when you turn on a light and you  have solar panels that extra electricity  needs to come from somewhere so it comes  through the grid and if the grid is down  then it doesn't it doesn't come from  anywhere and as part of the path forward  on this I mean Elon Musk from Tesla has  talked about the home battery systems  that they're working on that within a  few years he says will be  affordable I mean is is that the type of  solution that you see is the most likely  to this problem I think it's very hard  to see the future because i think that  people really like the idea of being  part of a larger electricity system it's  it was very rare in my research that i  found people that wanted to wanted some  kind of energy wanted to be isolated all  the time they're happy to produce for  the grid solar panels in particular like  very happy to produce for the grid  during the day time to to draw from the  grid at night time so i think that that  there is a possibility certainly with  those home battery storage systems that  you could be energy independent all the  time so you wouldn't need the grid at  all but i think what's more likely is  that people will start to use those so  that they have power when the power is  out on the larger system in from and and  that was a lot of what was happening  when i was doing my research is that a  lot of the frustration i found with the  electricity system part of it was this  feeling of like we want more renewables  which is which is very hard to manage  and i think there's a lot of work going  in that direction by people who do  manage the grid right now but not fast  enough right there's there's a lot of  anger with utilities in particular for  not making that transition quickly  enough but the other big frustration was  my powers out all the time i have solar  panels why can't I just have my power  beyond like why do I have to also have a  diesel backup generator so this movement  from producing power independently but  also trying to be green at home and  wanting the larger electricity system to  be green as well so if we step aside a  little bit from the storage aspect of it  I'd love to talk a little bit about some  of the other ways in which the grid is  relatively susceptible to outside forces  Abby we we have mostly in this country  elevated power lines where anything from  whether to a squirrel can cause pretty  significant interruptions is that one of  the biggest sort of liabilities in the  American electrical grid right now and  you mean the the power lines being above  ground yeah it's certainly certainly  it's a it  in part because the greatest threat to  the grid right now is growing trees okay  if they were underground that would be  less of a problem it's really hard to  cut back all of the foliage that is just  up and coming over this huge nation  right it's always just going up and up  and up and sort of grabbing at power  lines and so yes it's not always the  answer to bury though if you have some  place like Florida with a high sort of a  high title um I don't know I think the  word is scaled but the water comes a  very high title ii or when when it's  very wet you don't want to bury you  don't want to bury lines and you don't  even want critical infrastructure at  ground level you want to start putting  stuff up you want to put your  substations on hills so for some places  it's quite it's quite a good idea to  bury lines in other places it's a less  good idea the grid is weirdly local so  we have three of them in the US and  they're each gigantic through these huge  machines are sort of one for the west  one for the east and one for Texas but  nevertheless it's also extremely local  in how it's designed how different  states have been keeping it up the kinds  of laws there are laws that are federal  but there are many laws that are very  particular to states or even two  counties or two municipalities and so  taking into account the weather in  particular places matters for answering  that question and but it's also  extremely expensive to bury lines yeah  and that also gets towards what sort of  underlying this which is a I don't know  if I would call it a jurisdictional  issue but certainly one of ownership  which is that there's not like a single  entity that owns or controls or  maintains the grid right i mean as your  sort of talking about you're talking  about municipalities and a lot of  smaller entities that are part of this  grid mm-hmm and even manage their own  grid and there are many municipal like  Los Angeles for example has it manages  its own grid even though that grid is  connected into the entire gigantic grid  which covers the whole of the West it's  still that particular piece of it like  where they're buying electricity from  how much they're paying for it who's  maintaining the lines all of that is is  managed by the municipality and some  municipal grids are very very small I I  talk about in the book Rancho Cucamonga  which has one street light but they have  a municipal power windows municipal  our governance and people again are sort  of organizing suburbs etc as they get  frustrated with the the rate of change  to a more renewable renewably powered  system they are they'll just get towns  or suburbs together and they'll  disaggregate it's called from the big  grid and take over their own little  piece of it to manage it for themselves  and so that's sort of that so that  happens legally in the state so it's  both big and you know very very small  very very local and absolutely you know  nationally gigantic and I think that's  where you see a lot of the excitement  about what's happening right now I know  most people don't really see the grid or  think about the grid but it's it's  changing radically at this exact moment  in time uh and lots of people are  causing that change to happen in  different ways well it is a hugely  important issue particularly when we  have the conversation about when and how  will we get off of fossil fuels it's  something that that could not be more  important again we've been talking about  the book the grid the fraying wires  between Americans and our energy future  and we've been speaking with the book's  author Gretchen baki thanks so much for  talking to us about this yeah my  pleasure thank you
 

